window.HDH_SEARCH=[{"t":"Getting started","s":"","u":"docs/getting-started/","x":"Expenity keeps your money, bills and goals in one place, on every device you use: iPhone, iPad, Android, Windows, Mac and Linux, with nothing to download from a store. This guide takes about five minutes, and every step after the account can be skipped."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"1. Create an account (or sign in)","u":"docs/getting-started/#1-create-an-account-or-sign-in","x":"Open the app at expenity.github.io/expenity/app/. The first screen asks you to create an account or sign in. You use a passkey , which is your fingerprint, face or device PIN, so there is no password to remember or lose. Type your email, press Create account , and your device asks you to confirm. !The welcome screen. Expenity then shows ten recovery codes . Save them somewhere safe. They are how you get back in if you lose your passkeys, and they are shown only once. See Account and sync. Not ready for an account? Press Try without an account . Your data then stays in this browser only, and yo"},{"t":"Getting started","s":"2. Add Expenity to your home screen","u":"docs/getting-started/#2-add-expenity-to-your-home-screen","x":"The next screen shows, with a short animation, how to add Expenity to your home screen on iPhone, Android or a computer, so it opens like an app. Choose your device, follow the four steps, then press I added it , or Skip for now . More in Install on your device. !The install guide."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"3. Set it up","u":"docs/getting-started/#3-set-it-up","x":"A short setup asks a few questions. Each one can be skipped, and you can change every answer later. - Your name , used in the greeting. - Your main currency , starting with EUR. Totals and reports use it. - What you want help with , such as tracking spending, saving for a trip, or cutting subscriptions. You can choose more than one, and one is already selected to show how. Expenity uses this to suggest things that fit you. See Budgets, goals and income. - Your first goals , if you chose to save for something. Pick as many ideas as you like (trip, car, gift, home, emergency fund) or add your ow"},{"t":"Getting started","s":"4. Add your first expense","u":"docs/getting-started/#4-add-your-first-expense","x":"Press the green + button, choose Expense , type the amount on the keypad, add a description, pick a category and a wallet, and press the check mark. More about adding money. !The Home screen."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"Find your way around","u":"docs/getting-started/#find-your-way-around","x":"- Home : your total balance, income and expenses this month, ideas for you, wallets, what is due soon. - Activity : every transaction, by month, with search and filters. - Bills : what is coming, as a list or a calendar. - Reports : charts and tables. See Reports. - Plan (budgets and goals): spending limits and savings targets, in its own tab at the bottom (and in the sidebar on a tablet or computer). - Activity → Income → Recurring income : salary and other regular income. - Home → Ideas : suggestions and your aims. - More : wallets, categories, reminders, backup, themes, your account."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"Where to go next","u":"docs/getting-started/#where-to-go-next","x":"- Add expenses, income and bills - Wallets and currencies - Budgets, goals and income - Shared wallets"},{"t":"Getting started","s":"A quick tour, at your pace","u":"docs/getting-started/#a-quick-tour-at-your-pace","x":"After choosing a look, an animated tour explains Home, Activity, Bills, Plan (budgets and goals) and Reports. On phones, the swipe guide shows how to reveal row actions: swiping never pays or deletes anything by itself. All optional setup steps can be skipped. Existing accounts that have completed setup return to their data after signing in. Blur, floating cards and transitions follow your animation setting and your device's reduced-motion preference. Built-in wallet and category labels follow the selected language. Names you write yourself, including saved transaction names, stay as written. "},{"t":"Getting started","s":"Help is always one tap away","u":"docs/getting-started/#help-is-always-one-tap-away","x":"Look for the pulsing ? at the top of a screen: it opens a short animated tutorial in a popup without taking you away from what you are doing. All tutorials are in More → Help & about → Tutorials , and Contact us is next to them. See Tutorials and help."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/","x":"Expenity is a web app. You can use it in a browser tab, or add it to your home screen so it opens full screen with its own icon, like any other app. The first-run flow shows this with a short animation; these are the same steps. !The install guide in the app."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"Works on your devices","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#works-on-your-devices","x":"Expenity runs in a current browser on iPhone, iPad, Android phones and tablets, Windows, Mac and Linux . There is no app store and nothing to download: you open it in the browser, and you can install it so it opens full screen and works offline. Device Browser How to install Good to know iPhone and iPad Safari Share, then Add to Home Screen Notifications when the app is closed need the home-screen version Android Chrome Install app Receipt scanning uses the camera or a photo from your gallery Windows, Mac, Linux Chrome or Edge The install icon in the address bar Safari on a Mac can add it to t"},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"iPhone and iPad","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#iphone-and-ipad","x":"1. Open Expenity in Safari . 2. Tap the Share button. 3. Choose Add to Home Screen . 4. Tap Add , then open Expenity from your home screen. 💡 Real-life example Eleni adds Expenity to her iPhone home screen and opens it like any other app, with no app store."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"Android","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#android","x":"1. Open Expenity in Chrome . 2. Open the menu (⋮). 3. Choose Install app (or Add to Home screen ) and confirm. If your browser offers it, the install step in the app has an Install now button that does this for you."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"Computer","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#computer","x":"In Chrome or Edge, click the install icon at the right end of the address bar, or open the menu and choose Install Expenity . In Safari on a Mac: File → Add to Dock."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"Offline","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#offline","x":"After the first visit the app files are stored on your device, so Expenity opens without a connection. Changes you make offline are saved on the device and sync when you are back online. If a new version is available, it updates the next time you open the app with a connection. 💡 Real-life example On a flight without internet, Petros adds his expenses as usual. They sync when he lands."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"Several devices","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#several-devices","x":"Sign in with the same account on each device. Your wallets, transactions, bills, budgets, goals and settings follow you. How the look is set (theme, animations, language) is remembered per device. See Account and sync. 💡 Real-life example Anna uses her phone on the go and her computer for monthly reports. It is the same data on both."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"","u":"docs/add-money/","x":"Everything starts with the green + button. It offers four things: an expense , an income , a transfer between wallets, and a bill ."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Add an expense or income","u":"docs/add-money/#add-an-expense-or-income","x":"The sheet is built around the amount, so the most important thing comes first. 1. Choose Expense , Income or Transfer at the top. 2. Tap the amount to open the keypad. Enter the value, then press ✓ Done to collapse it. 3. Add a description if you like, for example \"Lunch\". 4. Pick a category . Common ones are shown; More opens the rest, including your own categories. 5. Pick the wallet . 6. Pick the date : Today, Yesterday, or another date. Note adds a longer note. 7. Press the check mark. !The add sheet with the keypad. 💡 Real-life example Nikos pays €42.30 at the supermarket on his way home"},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Or type a sentence","u":"docs/add-money/#or-type-a-sentence","x":"Press Type it instead and write what happened, in English or Greek: - Coffee 3.50 yesterday - Salary 1500 income - Groceries 42,30 - Καφές 3,50 χθες Expenity fills the amount, description, date and type, and shows chips so you can see what it understood. 💡 Real-life example Waiting in a queue, Maria types Coffee 3.50 yesterday for the coffee she forgot to log. Expenity picks out the name, the amount and yesterday's date, she checks the chips and saves."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Amounts show their separators","u":"docs/add-money/#amounts-show-their-separators","x":"As you type an amount, the field adds the separators for your language right inside it: 1234567.5 becomes 1,234,567.5 in English and 1.234.567,5 in Greek. You can type either a dot or a comma for the decimals."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Smart detection (Advanced)","u":"docs/add-money/#smart-detection-advanced","x":"With Smart detection on, type a description such as Coffee with Anna and Expenity fills in the category, up to two tags and the wallet for you. A line under the description says what it filled in, with an Undo button. - It looks at your own earlier entries first, then at built-in words in English and Greek, then at your own category names. - It never overwrites something you chose yourself, and after Undo it stays off for that entry. - Tags come from a short shared list (Coffee, Groceries, Car, Home and so on) or your own tags, so similar things always get the same tag. - If you have one walle"},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"When a field is missing","u":"docs/add-money/#when-a-field-is-missing","x":"If you press save and something is missing, every missing field stays outlined in red until you fill it in. Once you fill one in, Expenity takes you to the next missing field, from top to bottom. 💡 Real-life example Giorgos presses Save with no amount and no category. Both stay outlined in red. He enters the amount and the screen moves to the category on its own. One tap later, the entry is saved."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Tags","u":"docs/add-money/#tags","x":"Tags are short labels you can add to any entry, such as Coffee , Work or Trip . Type several at once, separated by commas : Coffee, Work adds two tags. You can also tap a tag you already have. Find your entries again with the tag filter in Activity, and manage all your tags in More → Money organisation → Tags . Reuse the tags you already have instead of making a new one for each variation. Groceries and Grocery would become two separate tags. 💡 Real-life example On a business trip, Petros tags each payment Trip, Work . At the end of the month he filters Activity by Work and sends his employer"},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Transfers","u":"docs/add-money/#transfers","x":"A transfer moves money between two of your wallets, for example from the bank to cash. It is not an expense. If the wallets use different currencies, Expenity asks how much arrived in the second one. A transfer works between two private wallets, or between two wallets in the same shared wallet, not across the two. 💡 Real-life example Anna moves €200 from her bank wallet to her cash wallet before a weekend away. A transfer keeps both balances right and does not count as spending."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Bills","u":"docs/add-money/#bills","x":"A bill is something that comes up again and again, like rent or Netflix. Press + and choose Bill . - Type it in a sentence : Netflix 15.99 monthly on the 3rd , Rent €850 every month on 1st , Electricity ~60 monthly 15th (a tilde means the amount changes each time). - Pick a template : Rent, Electricity, Water, Internet, Mobile phone, Netflix, Spotify, Car insurance, Credit card, Gym, Property tax, School fees. Greek templates such as ΔΕΗ, ΕΥΔΑΠ and ΕΝΦΙΑ are included. - Or fill in the form : name, the wallet it is paid from, amount, next due date, how often it repeats (once, weekly, every 2 we"},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Mark a bill paid","u":"docs/add-money/#mark-a-bill-paid","x":"On the Bills screen, Paid records the payment as an expense in the wallet and moves a repeating bill to its next date. You can Undo right after. For a bill with a variable amount, Expenity asks how much you paid. Other actions, from the ⋯ menu on a bill: Snooze (to tomorrow or for 3 days), Skip this time , open the payment link, copy the payment reference, edit and delete. 💡 Real-life example The electricity bill of €64 is due on the 12th of every month. Dimitra adds it once as a monthly bill. Each month Expenity reminds her, she swipes it right to mark it Paid, and the payment is recorded as"},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Swipe and pull","u":"docs/add-money/#swipe-and-pull","x":"On a phone, swiping a row slides it to show buttons. Nothing happens until you tap a button . Tapping anywhere else closes the row, and a row you leave open slides back by itself after two seconds. On a tablet or computer there is no swiping: click a row and its details open with the same Edit, Delete and Paid buttons. - On a transaction : swipe left to reveal Edit and Delete . - On a bill : swipe right to reveal Paid , or left to reveal Edit and Delete . - Pull the screen down to sync now (when you are signed in). The setup shows this in a short animation. !The Activity screen. 💡 Real-life e"},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Edit and delete","u":"docs/add-money/#edit-and-delete","x":"Tap any row to see its details, edit it or delete it. On a tablet or computer the details show in a side panel. In a shared wallet, the details also say who added it, and a viewer can look but not change."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Amount entry and reusable names","u":"docs/add-money/#amount-entry-and-reusable-names","x":"The number pad starts closed . Tap the amount, enter a value, then press ✓ Done to keep the amount and collapse the pad. This does not save the transaction. Text fields also show a ✓ Done control to dismiss the phone keyboard. Press the checkmark at the top only when you are ready to save the whole transaction. To reuse a name, enter a description and tick Save this name for next time before saving. The next time you add the same kind of transaction, expand Saved names and tap one. Its name and category are reused; the amount, date and wallet remain under your control. Expenses and income have"},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Reference photos","u":"docs/add-money/#reference-photos","x":"While signed in, choose Attach photo on an expense, income, transfer, bill, wallet, budget, goal or recurring income. JPEG, PNG and WebP work; HEIC depends on browser decoding support. The original file must be under 20 MB. Expenity re-encodes the photo as JPEG, removes original metadata, resizes the longest side to at most 1,280 pixels and compresses it to 96 KB or less. Save the item after uploading. A paperclip marks a photo; open the item and choose View photo . Editing also offers Replace and Remove. Photos require an account and internet to upload or view. Up to 100 uploads fit in an acc"},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Press and hold","u":"docs/add-money/#press-and-hold","x":"Press and hold an entry or bill for half a second and a menu opens with Edit , Duplicate and Delete (and Paid for bills). The menu also shows a small animation: you do not have to hold, dragging the row sideways does the same. !The press-and-hold menu. {{tut:hold}} 💡 Real-life example At a café, Elena holds the last entry for half a second and a menu offers Edit, Duplicate and Delete. Duplicate saves her typing the same round of drinks again."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Scan, and look before you add twice","u":"docs/add-money/#scan-and-look-before-you-add-twice","x":"Hold the + button to scan a receipt: see Receipts, photos and your picture. If you add something that matches an entry from the last few days, the duplicate guard asks before saving: see Daily number, targets and streaks. Deleted something by mistake? See Search, filters, drafts and recently deleted. {{tut:quick}} For split category amounts, partial repayments and worked examples, see Split expenses and reimbursements. 💡 Real-life example Maria scans a supermarket receipt: the merchant, total and date are read on her phone. When she adds a near-identical entry the same day by mistake, the dup"},{"t":"Tutorials and help","s":"","u":"docs/tutorials-and-help/","x":"Expenity teaches itself. Every feature has a short animated tutorial that you can watch at any time, and a small help icon that opens the right one without taking you away from what you are doing."},{"t":"Tutorials and help","s":"The help icon","u":"docs/tutorials-and-help/#the-help-icon","x":"On screens that have a tutorial you will see a round, pulsing ? at the top of the screen (and in the header of sheets such as the entry form). It rings like a bell every few seconds so it is easy to spot. !The help popup over the Home screen. Tap it and the tutorial opens in a popup on top of your screen . Nothing closes and nothing is lost: if you were halfway through an expense, it is still there when you close the popup. At the bottom of the popup you can: - open All tutorials and pick another one without leaving the popup, or step through them with the arrows; - choose Never show this help"},{"t":"Tutorials and help","s":"Turn help icons on or off","u":"docs/tutorials-and-help/#turn-help-icons-on-or-off","x":"More → Help & about → Help icons has one switch for all help icons, one switch for each screen, and Show every help icon again to bring them all back. The same master switch is on the Tutorials page."},{"t":"Tutorials and help","s":"The tutorials page","u":"docs/tutorials-and-help/#the-tutorials-page","x":"More → Help & about → Tutorials lists every tutorial with a small description. New ones carry a NEW badge. Open one to watch it loop, read the three steps under it, and use the arrows to go through them all in order. !The list of tutorials. Tutorials include scanning receipts, swiping rows, press and hold, adding in one sentence, your first entry, wallets, bills, repeating income, budgets and goals, reports, notifications, themes and the pencil, features and plans, profile picture, editing Home, the duplicate guard, the daily number, search and filters, recently deleted, streaks, price-change "},{"t":"Tutorials and help","s":"The welcome tour","u":"docs/tutorials-and-help/#the-welcome-tour","x":"The first-run setup includes animated steps for the things people ask about most: scanning a receipt, adding an entry in one sentence, a few handy extras, and the help icon itself. !Handy extras in the first-run setup. !The setup step that introduces the help icon. Dismiss the tour or replay it from the Tutorials page: Replay the welcome tour ."},{"t":"Tutorials and help","s":"Right-click menu","u":"docs/tutorials-and-help/#right-click-menu","x":"On a computer, right-click an entry or a bill to get a small menu with Edit , Duplicate and Delete (and Paid for a bill). Right-click on empty space to add an Expense , Income or Bill , or to open Search or Reports . Text fields keep the browser's own menu, and holding Shift while you right-click also shows it. Turn the menu off in More → Make it yours → Features ."},{"t":"Tutorials and help","s":"Share Expenity","u":"docs/tutorials-and-help/#share-expenity","x":"In More → About and help , Share the love opens your phone's share sheet so you can send the link in a message. On a computer, or if the share sheet is not available, Copy link copies a short friendly message with the link. After about a week of use, a card on Home also offers it once. Not now hides it for a month. See Privacy and your data for what is counted. 💡 Real-life example Maria tells her sister how she finally keeps track of her bills. She opens About, presses Share the love and sends her sister the link."},{"t":"Tutorials and help","s":"What's new","u":"docs/tutorials-and-help/#whats-new","x":"After an update, a small window shows what changed. Press Got it to close it, or View changes for the full list."},{"t":"Tutorials and help","s":"Still stuck?","u":"docs/tutorials-and-help/#still-stuck","x":"Use More → Help & about → Contact us . See Contact us. For split category amounts, partial repayments and worked examples, see Split expenses and reimbursements."},{"t":"Wallets and currencies","s":"","u":"docs/wallets-and-currencies/","x":"A wallet is a place where money is: cash, a bank account, a card, a savings account. Every transaction belongs to a wallet, and a wallet's balance is its opening balance plus income, minus expenses, with transfers in and out. !Your wallets."},{"t":"Wallets and currencies","s":"Add and edit wallets","u":"docs/wallets-and-currencies/#add-and-edit-wallets","x":"Open More → Wallets , or tap a wallet on Home. Each wallet has a name, a type (cash, bank account, card, savings, other), a currency and an opening balance , which is what was in it when you started. You can archive a wallet to hide it. A wallet that already has transactions is archived instead of deleted, so history is never lost. 💡 Real-life example Maria keeps a Cash wallet, a Bank wallet and a Savings wallet, each starting with the money it really holds, so every payment is recorded against the right one."},{"t":"Wallets and currencies","s":"Your main currency","u":"docs/wallets-and-currencies/#your-main-currency","x":"You choose a main currency in the setup, and can change it in More → Reminders and calendar → Main currency . Totals on Home and in Reports are shown in it. 💡 Real-life example Dimitra lives in Greece and sets the euro as her main currency, so totals and reports are always in euros."},{"t":"Wallets and currencies","s":"Wallets in other currencies","u":"docs/wallets-and-currencies/#wallets-in-other-currencies","x":"A wallet can use any currency. A bill or transaction takes the currency of its wallet. When your wallets use several currencies, totals are converted to your main currency using daily exchange rates, and shown with ≈ to say they are approximate. Expenity never adds different currencies together as plain numbers. Rates are the European Central Bank reference rates, fetched through the Expenity API once a day. Only the public rates are requested: nothing about you is sent. If rates cannot be loaded, other currencies are listed on their own lines instead of being converted. 💡 Real-life example N"},{"t":"Wallets and currencies","s":"Transfers","u":"docs/wallets-and-currencies/#transfers","x":"To move money between wallets, press + and choose Transfer . If the two wallets use different currencies, you also enter the amount received. Transfers are not counted as income or expenses."},{"t":"Wallets and currencies","s":"Money set aside for goals","u":"docs/wallets-and-currencies/#money-set-aside-for-goals","x":"When you add money to a goal, it is taken from a wallet you choose, so the wallet balance drops. That money is not counted as spending in your reports or budgets. 💡 Real-life example Eleni sets aside €50 a month for a holiday goal. The goal's ring fills up while that money stays separate from her everyday balance."},{"t":"Wallets and currencies","s":"Wallet colors","u":"docs/wallets-and-currencies/#wallet-colors","x":"Every wallet has a color. By default it follows your theme: the first wallet uses the theme's accent color and the others step around the color wheel from it, so they change when you change the theme. To choose your own, open More → Wallets , pick a wallet and choose a color (or Theme color to go back to the default, or the + swatch for any color you like). 💡 Real-life example Anna gives her work card a blue color and her personal card a green one, so she sees at a glance which wallet an entry belongs to."},{"t":"Split expenses and reimbursements","s":"","u":"docs/split-expenses-and-reimbursements/","x":"Both features belong to Advanced . You can also enable them individually in a Custom mix under More → Features. They are free features, not paid subscriptions. Turning them off does not remove records or change their calculations."},{"t":"Split expenses and reimbursements","s":"Split one payment between categories","u":"docs/split-expenses-and-reimbursements/#split-one-payment-between-categories","x":"Example: you spend €60 at a supermarket: €40 on food and €20 on household shopping. 1. Add an Expense and enter the full €60 payment, wallet and date. 2. Open Split expenses and enable Split across categories . 3. Set Food & groceries · €40 and Shopping · €20 . Add or remove category rows as needed. 4. Save. Every allocation must be positive and the amounts must add up exactly to €60. A difference of one cent prevents saving. Your wallet decreases by €60 once . Activity shows one expense, with a split icon. Open it to see every category amount. Category reports and budgets use €40 and €20 resp"},{"t":"Split expenses and reimbursements","s":"Track money someone will repay","u":"docs/split-expenses-and-reimbursements/#track-money-someone-will-repay","x":"This is money you paid on behalf of a person or organisation, not a refund from a shop and not salary. Example: you pay €90 for dinner. Alex owes you €60 . Your eventual personal cost is €30 . 1. Add the €90 expense. Open Reimbursements , enable Someone will repay me , enter Alex and the expected €60 , and save. 2. When Alex pays €20, open the original expense → Record repayment . Choose the receiving wallet and date, review the amount and confirm. 3. The wallet increases by €20; the expense shows Received €20 · Still owed €40 . 4. Record the final €40 when it actually arrives. The remaining a"},{"t":"Split expenses and reimbursements","s":"Reports, dates and data safety","u":"docs/split-expenses-and-reimbursements/#reports-dates-and-data-safety","x":"Reports treat repayments as negative spending on the date received , not earnings. For a split expense, the credit is allocated proportionally between its categories, with the final category absorbing any rounding cent. A reimbursement received in a later month reduces that later month's spending. That period can show a negative net expense; signed amounts remain available in tables. Balances use the original payment and actual receipts once each. Deleting an original expense does not delete money already received: linked receipts keep a category snapshot and remain in history, with an explana"},{"t":"Budgets, goals and income","s":"","u":"docs/budgets-goals-income/","x":"Budgets & goals contains two tabs: Budgets and Goals . Find it on Home, in More, or in the tablet/desktop sidebar. A dot pulses briefly until your first visit. Recurring income lives in Activity → Income → Recurring income . Ideas lives on Home , with See all to expand the full list and change your aims."},{"t":"Budgets, goals and income","s":"Budgets","u":"docs/budgets-goals-income/#budgets","x":"A budget is a limit for a category (or for everything) over a period. 1. Open Budgets & goals → Budgets → Add budget . 2. Choose a category, or Everything . 3. Set the limit, and the period: monthly, weekly or yearly. 4. Optionally turn on Carry over what is left from the last period . !Budgets with progress bars. Each budget shows what you spent, what is left, a progress bar that turns amber at 80 percent and red at 100 percent, and an amount you can spend per day for the rest of the period. 💡 Real-life example Petros sets a €250 monthly budget for eating out. When he has spent 80% the bar w"},{"t":"Budgets, goals and income","s":"Goals","u":"docs/budgets-goals-income/#goals","x":"A goal is something you save for: a trip, a car, a gift, an emergency fund. 1. Open Budgets & goals → Goals → Add goal . 2. Give it a name, a target amount, an optional deadline and an icon. 3. Press Add money on the goal to move money in from a wallet, or Take out to move it back. !Goals with progress rings. The ring shows progress. Expenity estimates the date you will reach the goal from how you have been saving over the last 90 days, and, when you set a deadline, how much per month would get you there. You get a small celebration at 25, 50, 75 and 100 percent. The Goals tab also shows a sav"},{"t":"Budgets, goals and income","s":"Recurring income","u":"docs/budgets-goals-income/#recurring-income","x":"Open Activity → Income → Recurring income → Add recurring income . Add your salary or other regular income as a schedule: a name, the wallet it arrives in, the amount, the next date and how often. When it arrives, press Received . Expenity records the income in the wallet and moves the schedule to the next date (you can undo). Expected income feeds the forecast. 💡 Real-life example Giorgos's salary arrives on the 28th. As recurring income it is recorded as received on time, without anyone having to confirm it."},{"t":"Budgets, goals and income","s":"Ideas for you","u":"docs/budgets-goals-income/#ideas-for-you","x":"In the setup you can say what you want from Expenity, and you can change it any time in Home → Ideas → See all: track spending, save for something, build savings, cut subscriptions, spend less on everyday things, never miss a bill, pay off debt. Expenity then looks at your own data, on your device, and offers specific ideas, for example: - A subscription you could cancel, with what it costs a year and how much of a goal that would cover. - Delivery or eating out over the last 30 days, and what cooking at home half of the time would save. Expenity recognises these from the names you type, such "},{"t":"Daily number, targets and streaks","s":"","u":"docs/spending-helpers/","x":"Expenity does a little thinking for you. These helpers turn your records into something you can act on today."},{"t":"Daily number, targets and streaks","s":"You can spend today","u":"docs/spending-helpers/#you-can-spend-today","x":"The daily number card on Home answers one question: how much can I spend today without worrying? It starts from your balance, subtracts the bills due until your next income, your budgets and any reserve you have set, then spreads what is left over the days to come. The ring empties as you spend. Tap the arrow for the full breakdown, or open the Safe to spend details. !Home with the daily number, an income target and a streak. 💡 Real-life example On the 20th, Maria opens Home and sees €18 she can safely spend today, after her bills and budgets are taken into account."},{"t":"Daily number, targets and streaks","s":"Set your own targets","u":"docs/spending-helpers/#set-your-own-targets","x":"The automatic figure is a suggestion. Tap the pencil on the card to set your own. !Choosing a spending and an income target. - Spending: keep Automatic , or choose a Daily , Weekly or Monthly target and an amount. The card then shows what is left to spend in that period, or how far over you are. Weeks run Monday to Sunday. - Income target: optional. Choose daily, weekly or monthly and an amount, and a progress bar shows what you have earned so far, with \"Target reached\" when you get there. Targets are saved with your settings, so they follow you to your other devices. They count expenses and i"},{"t":"Daily number, targets and streaks","s":"Streaks","u":"docs/spending-helpers/#streaks","x":"The flame counts consecutive days within budget . If you have no budgets, it counts days you spent nothing. The seven dots show the last week, and Expenity keeps your best streak and the number of no-spend days this month. Overspend once and the flame resets: start again tomorrow. {{tut:streak}} 💡 Real-life example Eleni stays within her daily budget nine days in a row and watches her flame grow. Overspending once resets it, so she starts again tomorrow."},{"t":"Daily number, targets and streaks","s":"Duplicate guard","u":"docs/spending-helpers/#duplicate-guard","x":"Before saving an expense or income, Expenity checks the last few days for an identical entry: same name, amount and wallet. If it finds one, it pauses and shows you what it found, with the date. Choose Add anyway if it is real, or cancel. {{tut:dup}} 💡 Real-life example On a slow connection Petros taps Save twice. The duplicate guard stops him and shows the identical entry from a minute ago."},{"t":"Daily number, targets and streaks","s":"Price-change alerts","u":"docs/spending-helpers/#price-change-alerts","x":"When you pay a bill and the amount is higher than the previous payment, Expenity tells you by how much, and a small red chip such as +12% stays on the bill so you can review it. It compares payments you recorded, so variable bills such as electricity are exactly where it helps. {{tut:price}} 💡 Real-life example The internet bill goes from €25 to €29. Anna sees a red chip and a note on the bill, and calls her provider."},{"t":"Daily number, targets and streaks","s":"Switching them on or off","u":"docs/spending-helpers/#switching-them-on-or-off","x":"The daily number, duplicate guard and price alerts are part of Pro , and streaks are part of Advanced . See Features and plans."},{"t":"Search, filters, drafts and recently deleted","s":"","u":"docs/find-and-recover/","x":""},{"t":"Search, filters, drafts and recently deleted","s":"Search and filters","u":"docs/find-and-recover/#search-and-filters","x":"In Activity , tap the magnifier to search by name, note, tag or category. Under the search box, Filters narrows the list further: - a minimum and maximum amount ; - a date range . When a range is set, the list covers those dates across months instead of one month at a time; - a tag , and the type and wallet chips as before. !Activity with an amount filter. The number next to Filters shows how many are active, and Clear filters removes them all. {{tut:filters}} 💡 Real-life example Looking for a payment, Kostas filters Activity to amounts over €100 in March and finds the dentist's bill in secon"},{"t":"Search, filters, drafts and recently deleted","s":"Recently deleted","u":"docs/find-and-recover/#recently-deleted","x":"Deleted an entry or bill by mistake? More → Account & data → Recently deleted keeps them for 30 days . Tap Restore to bring one back. A restored item is added as a new one with the same details. You can also empty the list. !The recently deleted list. {{tut:trash}} 💡 Real-life example Dimitra deletes an entry by mistake. Within 30 days she restores it from Recently deleted."},{"t":"Search, filters, drafts and recently deleted","s":"Drafts","u":"docs/find-and-recover/#drafts","x":"Started an expense and your phone died, or you closed the app by accident? Expenity saves what you typed on your device as soon as you change anything, such as adding an amount. When you open the app, Home points out the unfinished entry with a Continue button, and you can dismiss it with the cross. !A draft pointed out on Home. More → Account & data → Drafts lists every unfinished entry with Continue and Dismiss . Drafts stay for 24 hours and live only on the device where you started them. Continue opens the entry form with everything you had typed, ready to submit. 💡 Real-life example A pho"},{"t":"Search, filters, drafts and recently deleted","s":"Reset account","u":"docs/find-and-recover/#reset-account","x":"If you want a completely clean slate, More → Account and sync → Reset account removes all your records, wallets, budgets, goals and photos and resets your settings, as if you had just joined. If you have an account, this happens on all your devices: each one is emptied the next time it syncs, and nothing comes back. Your account and passkeys stay. It asks for confirmation and a passkey, and you are taken through the setup again, starting with your name. The same card has a Download a backup first button. If you have shared wallets, Expenity shows what happens to each one. When you agree, you l"},{"t":"Receipts, photos and your picture","s":"","u":"docs/receipts-and-photos/","x":""},{"t":"Receipts, photos and your picture","s":"Scan a receipt","u":"docs/receipts-and-photos/#scan-a-receipt","x":"Photograph a receipt and Expenity reads the merchant, the total and the date on your device , in English and Greek. Nothing is uploaded for recognition. - Hold the + button for half a second, take the photo, or choose one from your library. On a computer, open Add , then Scan receipt . - Review the fields. You choose what to apply: the name, the amount, the currency, the date, and whether to keep the photo. - Scanned entries show a small receipt icon. Open one and choose Re-scan receipt to read it again, for example with a better photo. !The scan step in the first-run setup. {{tut:scan}} The s"},{"t":"Receipts, photos and your picture","s":"Photos on entries","u":"docs/receipts-and-photos/#photos-on-entries","x":"Attach a small reference photo or screenshot to an expense, income, bill, wallet or goal: open it, then More → Attach photo . Photos are shrunk to about 1000 pixels and 60 KB before upload, and a green check confirms it. An entry can show both a receipt icon and a photo icon. Tap the photo icon to view it. Photos need an account and a connection, and you can delete them all from More → Data . {{tut:attach}} 💡 Real-life example Anna photographs the warranty receipt of a new washing machine and attaches it to the payment, so it is there the day she needs it."},{"t":"Receipts, photos and your picture","s":"Your profile picture","u":"docs/receipts-and-photos/#your-profile-picture","x":"Choose More → Profile picture to pick one of 20 characters (funny, animated or minimal) or use your own photo. Your choice is only a preview until you press Save , and a green check marks the one you picked. A photo is cropped to a small round picture and saved with your settings. When you are offline, the picture gets a soft pulse so you can tell. !Choosing a profile picture."},{"t":"Reports","s":"","u":"docs/reports/","x":"Reports turn your transactions into charts and tables. Open them with the chart icon in the top bar of Home, Activity, Bills and Plan, or from More . The All view shows every report one after the other, each with an Open link, so you can scroll through everything. The buttons under the filters (each with an icon) open one report on its own. Pick a period at the top (This month, Last month, 3 months, This year, 12 months, or a custom range), then a tab. Filters narrow everything to a wallet, a category, only expenses or only income, or a word in the description. Goal money is left out of spendi"},{"t":"Reports","s":"Overview","u":"docs/reports/#overview","x":"Net worth, net result, income, expenses, savings rate and spending per day, each with an arrow and a percentage compared with the previous period of the same length. Below, the top categories and your biggest expense. 💡 Real-life example At the end of the month Nikos opens Reports and sees income, expenses, his savings rate and what he spent per day, all on one screen."},{"t":"Reports","s":"Spending","u":"docs/reports/#spending","x":"A donut chart by category. Tap a slice or a legend row to drill down into that category's transactions. Also: average spending by weekday, where the money goes (by description) and the biggest expenses. !Spending by category. 💡 Real-life example Anna taps the biggest slice of the donut and sees the entries behind it: three restaurant bills explain most of it."},{"t":"Reports","s":"Cash flow","u":"docs/reports/#cash-flow","x":"Income and expenses month by month, cumulative cash flow, and your savings rate by month."},{"t":"Reports","s":"Trends","u":"docs/reports/#trends","x":"Spending by month stacked by category (the top five and the rest), and how this month compares with last month. 💡 Real-life example Petros sees that his coffee spending has grown every month and decides to cut down."},{"t":"Reports","s":"Forecast","u":"docs/reports/#forecast","x":"The expected balance in 30, 60 and 90 days, drawn as a line. It uses your bills, your recurring income and a typical daily spend worked out from the last 60 days, and says so under the chart. It also shows the amount you can spend per day until the end of the month, the lowest point your balance reaches and the biggest payments coming up. !The forecast. 💡 Real-life example Before a big purchase, Elena checks the forecast, which includes her upcoming bills and expected income, and sees the lowest balance she should expect over the next months."},{"t":"Reports","s":"Subscriptions","u":"docs/reports/#subscriptions","x":"A table of your subscriptions with the monthly and yearly cost and the next charge, the total per year, and a note when a price went up. 💡 Real-life example Kostas lists his subscriptions and finds he pays €38 a month for services he barely uses. He cancels two of them."},{"t":"Reports","s":"Table","u":"docs/reports/#table","x":"Every transaction in the period, in a table you can sort by clicking a heading and group by category, wallet or month, with totals. CSV downloads it for a spreadsheet. Every chart has Show as a table , so the numbers are always available as text. On a tablet or computer the charts and tiles use the extra room. !Reports on a tablet. For split category amounts, partial repayments and worked examples, see Split expenses and reimbursements."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"","u":"docs/reminders/","x":"Expenity can remind you in three ways. The first one works like a normal app: a notification arrives even when Expenity is closed."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"1. Notifications when the app is closed","u":"docs/reminders/#1-notifications-when-the-app-is-closed","x":"With an account, Expenity sends you one summary a day at the hour you choose, listing the bills that are due soon or overdue. It arrives like any app notification, on the lock screen, even if Expenity is not open. 1. Sign in (an account is needed, because Expenity has to know which bills are due). 2. Open More → Reminders and calendar → Notifications on this device and press Turn on . Allow notifications when your browser asks. 3. Choose the time you want it. Send a test shows you what it looks like. Things to know: - iPhone and iPad: Apple only allows this for apps added to the Home Screen. A"},{"t":"Reminders","s":"2. Your calendar (works without an account)","u":"docs/reminders/#2-your-calendar-works-without-an-account","x":"This is the reminder to rely on. 1. Open More → Reminders and calendar . 2. Press Download calendar file . 3. Import the file into your calendar app. The file holds every open bill (not recurring income or budgets) as an all-day event, with its repeat rule and two alarms: at 09:00 on the reminder day you chose, and at 09:00 on the due day. Your calendar app then does the reminding on every device where that calendar syncs. How to import: - Google Calendar (on a computer): Settings → Import & export → Import, choose the file. - Apple Calendar : open the file, or File → Import, and choose a cale"},{"t":"Reminders","s":"3. Notifications while Expenity is open","u":"docs/reminders/#3-notifications-while-expenity-is-open","x":"In More → Reminders and calendar → Notifications while Expenity is open , press Turn on and allow notifications when your browser asks. When you open Expenity or keep it in a tab, you get one notification a day for bills that are due within their reminder window and are not snoozed. This does not work when the app is fully closed. For that, use notifications when the app is closed (above) or the calendar file."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"Choose how early","u":"docs/reminders/#choose-how-early","x":"In More → Reminders and calendar choose the Default reminder , from the day itself up to two weeks before. Each bill can override it in the Remind me field. 💡 Real-life example Rent matters most, so Maria asks to be reminded five days ahead for it and one day ahead for everything else."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"Snooze","u":"docs/reminders/#snooze","x":"In a bill's ⋯ menu, Snooze to tomorrow or Snooze 3 days stops its notifications for a while. 💡 Real-life example Petros cannot pay today, so he snoozes the reminder until Friday and it comes back then."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"What is coming","u":"docs/reminders/#what-is-coming","x":"Email reminders need a sending domain and are planned. Notifications on the lock screen and the calendar file cover the same need today."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"","u":"docs/shared-wallets/","x":"A shared wallet lets family or friends see and add to the same wallet from their own devices, for a household budget, a trip or a shared account. Only the wallet you share is visible to others: your other wallets, budgets and goals stay private. !The sharing sheet."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"Share a wallet","u":"docs/shared-wallets/#share-a-wallet","x":"You need an account (not guest mode). 1. Open More → Wallets and tap the wallet. 2. Press Share this wallet , then Start sharing . 3. Choose a role for the person you invite: Can edit or Can view . 4. Press Create invite . You get a code and a link. Send either one to the person, in a message or in person. An invite works once and expires after 7 days. You can see open invites and cancel them. You can share up to 5 wallets, with up to 12 people each. 💡 Real-life example Nikos and Eleni share a Household wallet. Both add the groceries and the bills, and both see the same balance."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"Join a shared wallet","u":"docs/shared-wallets/#join-a-shared-wallet","x":"Open the link you were sent, or go to More → Wallets → Join a shared wallet and type the code. You need an account, and Expenity asks you to sign in or create one first if you do not have one. The wallet and its history then appear on your device. 💡 Real-life example Eleni joins the Household wallet with the invite code Nikos sent her, and it appears on her phone straight away."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"Roles","u":"docs/shared-wallets/#roles","x":"Role Can do Owner Everything: invite people, change roles, remove people, stop sharing Can edit See everything in the wallet, add and change transactions and bills Can view See everything in the wallet, but not change it The server checks the role on every change, not only the app. Each transaction and bill shows who added it. 💡 Real-life example The grandparents get View only access to the family wallet so they can see the numbers, while the parents can edit."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"What is shared","u":"docs/shared-wallets/#what-is-shared","x":"The wallet, its transactions and its bills. Budgets, goals, recurring income and your settings are not shared. Transfers only work between two private wallets or between two wallets inside the same shared wallet."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"Privacy","u":"docs/shared-wallets/#privacy","x":"People in a shared wallet can see each other's email address and everything in that wallet. Tell the people you invite what they will see. 💡 Real-life example Everything else in Nikos's account stays private. Eleni sees only the wallet they share."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"Leave, remove, stop sharing","u":"docs/shared-wallets/#leave-remove-stop-sharing","x":"- A member can Leave this wallet . It is removed from their device and the others keep it. - The owner can Remove someone, who loses access and the data on their device. - The owner can Stop sharing and keep a copy : everyone else loses access, and you keep the wallet and all its transactions as a private wallet. You do not have to hunt for these buttons: More → Account and sync → Shared wallets lists every wallet you share, with Leave this wallet for the ones you joined and Stop sharing for the ones you own. If you delete your account, wallets you own and share are deleted for everyone, and y"},{"t":"Features and plans","s":"","u":"docs/features-and-plans/","x":"Expenity does a lot. You decide how much of it you see. More → Make it yours → Features lets you pick a ready-made plan, compare them in a table, or build your own mix with toggles. !The Features page with the three plans."},{"t":"Features and plans","s":"The three plans","u":"docs/features-and-plans/#the-three-plans","x":"- Basic : wallets, expenses, income, bills, categories and reports. Simple and quiet. - Pro : everything in Basic, plus repeating bills and income, budgets and goals, tags, quick entries, photos on entries, profile picture, notifications, shared wallets, the duplicate guard, the daily number, search filters, recently deleted, price-change alerts and draft reminders. - Advanced : everything in Pro, plus split expenses, reimbursements, receipt scanning, ideas, safe to spend, sounds, Home editing, theme editing and streaks. The first-run setup asks you to pick one, and Advanced is selected to sta"},{"t":"Features and plans","s":"All features by plan","u":"docs/features-and-plans/#all-features-by-plan","x":"<!-- features:start -- Basic has 8 features, Pro 23 and Advanced 34 . A tick means the feature is included. In your own mix you can switch any optional feature on or off. Feature Basic Pro Advanced :-: :-: :-: Always included Wallets ✓ ✓ ✓ Expenses, income and transfers ✓ ✓ ✓ Categories ✓ ✓ ✓ Bills with due dates ✓ ✓ ✓ Activity and search ✓ ✓ ✓ Reports ✓ ✓ ✓ Themes and languages ✓ ✓ ✓ Backup and export ✓ ✓ ✓ Added in Pro Repeating bills and income – ✓ ✓ Budgets and goals – ✓ ✓ Tags – ✓ ✓ Quick entries – ✓ ✓ Photos on entries – ✓ ✓ Profile picture – ✓ ✓ Notifications – ✓ ✓ Shared wallets – ✓ ✓ "},{"t":"Features and plans","s":"Compare and customise","u":"docs/features-and-plans/#compare-and-customise","x":"- What each plan includes opens a table with a tick for every feature in every plan. - Build your own has a toggle for every optional feature. Flip any of them and your plan becomes Custom . - Some things are always on: wallets, expenses, income and transfers, categories, bills with due dates, Activity and search, Reports, themes and languages, and backup and export. 💡 Real-life example Elena likes everything in Advanced except sounds, so she switches that one feature off in her own mix. Nothing is deleted."},{"t":"Features and plans","s":"Plans and tutorials","u":"docs/features-and-plans/#plans-and-tutorials","x":"Tutorials and help icons follow your plan: a feature that is switched off has no tutorial and no help icon. See Tutorials and help."},{"t":"Features and plans","s":"New in Advanced","u":"docs/features-and-plans/#new-in-advanced","x":"Split expenses and reimbursements add category allocations and partial repayments, with animated examples in setup and Tutorials. Smart detection fills in the category, tags and wallet from what you type."},{"t":"Themes and animations","s":"","u":"docs/themes-and-look/","x":"Expenity has eighteen themes, and every one has a pencil to adjust its colors, font and roundness. A theme changes more than colour: shapes, type, borders and a few details too. Pick one in More → Appearance and language , or during setup. !Six of the themes. Theme Look Emerald The default: fresh green, soft cards Minimal Black, white and hairlines Paper notes Ruled paper, handwriting, a sticky note for your balance Ledger An accounting book, serif type, double rules Retro terminal Green on black, monospace, scanlines Synthwave Neon pink and cyan with a grid horizon Blueprint White lines on bl"},{"t":"Themes and animations","s":"Animations","u":"docs/themes-and-look/#animations","x":"Expenity moves a little. The balance card on Home flips over when you tap it, to show this month in short. Numbers roll up, list rows and wallet cards fade in one after another, bars, rings and donut slices draw themselves, a row you delete fades away, a tab you press bounces, and a goal you reach throws confetti. Under Animations you can choose: - Match device : follows your device's \"reduce motion\" setting. - Full : all animations. - Reduced : a very short version of each. - Off : no animations at all. Some themes also have a little ambient motion behind the content: embers rising on Sunset,"},{"t":"Themes and animations","s":"Amberwave, Ion and your own palette","u":"docs/themes-and-look/#amberwave-ion-and-your-own-palette","x":"Amberwave combines amber neon with warm shadows; Ion uses electric cyan on deep blue. Together with Synthwave they have sparse falling dots with thin trails. The effect pauses when hidden and follows reduced/off motion settings. Select Your theme → Create your theme to choose background, cards, accent and text colours, corner shape and typography. Text must have readable contrast on both backgrounds before saving. Trails are optional. Custom theme choices are saved per device. The balance animation begins on launch and screen navigation, stays between zero and the true amount, and ends on the "},{"t":"Themes and animations","s":"Adjust any theme with the pencil","u":"docs/themes-and-look/#adjust-any-theme-with-the-pencil","x":"Every theme card has a small pencil . Tap it to change that theme's colors, font and roundness . Layout and positions never change, only the look, and your changes are saved straight away for that theme. The old separate custom theme has been replaced by this. !Editing a theme. {{tut:themes}} 💡 Real-life example Anna keeps the Emerald theme but makes the corners rounder and the accent color purple, with the pencil."},{"t":"Themes and animations","s":"Preview a theme on Home","u":"docs/themes-and-look/#preview-a-theme-on-home","x":"In the theme editor, Preview on Home shows your changes on the real Home screen. A panel with Apply and Keep editing stays above the tab bar. Apply saves the look. Keep editing takes you back to the editor with your changes. If you leave without applying, your previous theme comes back. 💡 Real-life example Before saving, Elena previews her new colors on the real Home screen to make sure the balance card is easy to read."},{"t":"Themes and animations","s":"Reports and sizes on Home","u":"docs/themes-and-look/#reports-and-sizes-on-home","x":"Where it went shows this month's spending by category at the bottom of Home. From More → Make it yours → Personalise Home you can add Trends and Subscriptions with Cards . Each card can be Small, Medium or Full, and only the sizes that look different are offered. Tap a report to open Reports. 💡 Real-life example Nikos makes Where it went full width and the daily number a small tile, so the top of Home shows what he checks most."},{"t":"Themes and animations","s":"A start screen dressed in your theme","u":"docs/themes-and-look/#a-start-screen-dressed-in-your-theme","x":"When you open the app, the start screen takes the colours of your theme. If loading takes a moment, a playful message keeps you company. It only appears when you open the app, not when you refresh the page. !The start screen in the Sunset theme. !The start screen in the Emerald theme. !The start screen in the Synthwave theme. !The start screen in the Pastel candy theme. 💡 Real-life example Eleni switches to a new theme for fun. The next morning, opening the app, she is greeted by her own colours before her day's numbers appear."},{"t":"Themes and animations","s":"Fonts","u":"docs/themes-and-look/#fonts","x":"Fonts are bundled with the app so it looks the same on every phone, tablet and computer, with full Greek support."},{"t":"Themes and animations","s":"Your picture and sounds","u":"docs/themes-and-look/#your-picture-and-sounds","x":"Choose a character or your own photo as your picture: see Receipts, photos and your picture. Optional sound effects can be turned on in More → Make it yours → Sounds (part of the Advanced plan)."},{"t":"Themes and animations","s":"Try the themes live","u":"docs/themes-and-look/#try-the-themes-live","x":"The Expenity website has the real app in an iPhone, iPad and computer frame, so you can switch themes and see them in action before you open the app."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"","u":"docs/account-and-sync/","x":"An account lets you use Expenity on all your devices and keeps your data safe if you lose one. It is free."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Passkeys, not passwords","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#passkeys-not-passwords","x":"You sign in with a passkey : your fingerprint, face or device PIN. Your device creates it, and Expenity never sees a password because there is none to forget, reuse or steal. Many devices sync passkeys between your own devices (iCloud Keychain, Google Password Manager, a password manager). - Create an account : type your email and confirm on your device. - Sign in : press Sign in with a passkey and choose your account. 💡 Real-life example Anna signs in with her fingerprint. There is no password to remember, reuse or leak."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Recovery codes","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#recovery-codes","x":"When you create an account you get ten one-time recovery codes . If you lose all your passkeys, enter your email and one code ( Lost your passkey? ) to get back in. You then add a new passkey, and every other device is signed out. Each code works once and they cannot be shown again, so keep them somewhere safe: a password manager or a printout. You can make a new set in More → Account and sync . 💡 Real-life example Maria's phone falls into the sea. She signs in on a new one with a recovery code and everything comes back."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Your devices","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#your-devices","x":"More → Account and sync lists your passkeys and the devices that are signed in. You can add another passkey, remove one (you always keep at least one) and sign a device out. 💡 Real-life example Nikos spots an old laptop in the device list and signs it out, so nobody can use it any more."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"How sync works","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#how-sync-works","x":"Expenity saves on your device first and syncs in the background, so it works offline. When you are back online, changes go up and other devices' changes come down. If two devices change the same item, the newest change wins for that item. Pull down on a screen to sync right now. The sync dot next to your avatar shows the state: green is synced. What syncs: wallets, transactions, bills, categories, budgets, goals, recurring income and some settings. Look settings (theme, animations, language) stay per device. 💡 Real-life example Maria adds an expense on her phone and a moment later it is on he"},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"If you clear your browser data","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#if-you-clear-your-browser-data","x":"Clearing cookies and site data signs you out, because the sign-in is kept in the browser's storage, and it removes anything Expenity stored on that device. Expenity asks the browser to keep its storage, but a manual clear always wins. - With an account , your data is safe on the server. Open Expenity and press Sign in with a passkey . Your passkey is kept by your device or password manager, not by the browser, so clearing the browser does not remove it. Everything syncs back. The email you gave at sign-up is your safety net: with it you can get back in if you also lose the passkey. - Without a"},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Check for updates","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#check-for-updates","x":"Expenity updates itself when you open it with a connection. To check right now, open More → Account and sync (or About and help ) and press Check for updates . If a newer version has been published, press Update now and the app reloads with it."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Sign out","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#sign-out","x":"Sign out removes the data from that device (it stays in your account). If something has not synced yet, Expenity tells you first. 💡 Real-life example Before handing an old tablet to a relative, Kostas signs out. His data stays in his account and leaves the tablet."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Delete your account","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#delete-your-account","x":"More → Account and sync → Delete my account deletes your account and all your synced data from the server. It asks you to confirm with your passkey. You are also asked to type DELETE . It cannot be undone. Wallets you own and share are deleted for everyone."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Email","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#email","x":"Expenity stores your email to identify your account. Email verification and email reminders are not switched on yet: they need a sending domain, which is planned."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Photo storage","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#photo-storage","x":"In Account, Delete all photos removes every photo you uploaded, including in shared wallets. Confirmation and a passkey check protect the action. Financial records remain. See photos and backups."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Reset account","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#reset-account","x":"Account → Reset account deletes all your records and settings on all your devices and takes you through the setup again, like a new user. Your account and passkeys stay. Other devices are emptied the next time they sync. Use Download a backup first on the same card if you might need your data. See Search, filters, drafts and recently deleted. You are asked to type RESET to confirm, and then to confirm with your passkey."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Your picture and offline status","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#your-picture-and-offline-status","x":"You can choose a profile picture in More . When the device is offline, the picture shows a soft pulse and a small cloud badge. Changes made offline are kept on the device and sync when you are back online."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/","x":""},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"What is stored, and where","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#what-is-stored-and-where","x":"On your device. Expenity saves financial records in your browser first, so it works offline. Without an account, that is the only place your data lives. On Expenity's server, if you create an account. To sync between your devices, your email address and your data (wallets, transactions, bills, budgets, goals, categories, recurring income and some settings) are stored on a Cloudflare Worker and database (D1). The connection is encrypted, and access needs your passkey. Expenity does not look at your data, sell it, share it or use it for advertising. As with most online services, it is not end-to"},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Back up and restore","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#back-up-and-restore","x":"Even with an account, you can keep your own copy. In More → Data and backup : - Download backup (JSON) saves your wallets, transactions, bills, budgets, goals and income schedules. - Restore a backup reads such a file and replaces the current data after you confirm. This is also how you move data from the guest mode of one browser to another. - Transactions as CSV exports your history for a spreadsheet. Without an account, clearing the site's data (or losing the device) removes your data, so back up regularly. 💡 Real-life example Every month Nikos downloads a backup file and keeps it in his o"},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Delete everything","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#delete-everything","x":"- Delete all my data (More → Data and backup) is shown only when you are not signed in. It removes everything from this browser, and because there is no account, it is gone for good. - Reset account (More → Account and sync) is the version for an account. It deletes all your data on every device, asks for your passkey, and keeps your account so you can start over. Use Download a backup first on the same card if you might need your data. - Delete my account (More → Account and sync) deletes your account and everything stored on the server. It asks for your passkey and cannot be undone. Wallets "},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Notifications while the app is open","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#notifications-while-the-app-is-open","x":"The in-app option shows them locally. Nothing is sent anywhere."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"The calendar file","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#the-calendar-file","x":"The calendar file you download contains your bill names, amounts and notes. When you import it into Google, Apple or Outlook calendar, it is stored by that service under its own privacy rules. Avoid sensitive details in notes if that matters to you."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Open source","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#open-source","x":"The app and the server are public, so you can check all of this: github.com/expenity/expenity."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Photos and saved names","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#photos-and-saved-names","x":"Saved names are optional account settings and sync with your financial data. Photos are compressed separately, stored in D1, and never cached by the app for offline viewing. They follow current wallet access. Delete your uploads in Account; other members’ uploads remain. JSON backups contain image references rather than the files."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Security","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#security","x":"We reviewed the app and its server end to end before sharing it. There are no secrets in the code or its history, and the dependencies are audited with no known vulnerabilities. The server limits how often anything can be tried, checks every input strictly, sends security headers, and only talks to genuine push services. The app was tested with hostile text in every place you can type, so what you enter is only ever shown as plain text. A content security policy limits what the app can load, and the app refuses to run inside another page. Sign-in uses passkeys, so there is no password to steal"},{"t":"Contact us","s":"","u":"docs/contact-us/","x":"Found a problem, have an idea or just want to say something? More → Help & about → Contact us sends your message straight to the person who makes Expenity. !The contact form."},{"t":"Contact us","s":"What you can send","u":"docs/contact-us/#what-you-can-send","x":"- Choose what it is about: Feedback , Problem , Idea or Question . - Write your message with simple formatting: bold, italic, underline, lists, a heading, a quote and links. - Attach up to four photos or screenshots . They are shrunk on your device before they are sent. - Add your email if you would like a reply. It is optional. - Leave Include app version, language and device type ticked: it helps us reproduce problems. Untick it if you prefer. You do not need an account to write to us."},{"t":"Contact us","s":"What happens to it","u":"docs/contact-us/#what-happens-to-it","x":"Your message goes straight to the developer's inbox, labelled as a message from the app. It is used to read and answer feedback and fix problems, and nothing is added to your Expenity data. See Privacy and your data."},{"t":"Contact us","s":"Other ways","u":"docs/contact-us/#other-ways","x":"Open an issue on GitHub. Before writing, a tutorial may already show the answer."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"","u":"docs/faq/","x":""},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Is Expenity free?","u":"docs/faq/#is-expenity-free","x":"Yes, and it is open source. There are no ads and no paid tiers today."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Does it work on my iPhone, Android phone, tablet and computer?","u":"docs/faq/#does-it-work-on-my-iphone-android-phone-tablet-and-computer","x":"Yes. It is a web app that runs in any modern browser, and you can add it to your home screen like an app: see Install on your device. Sign in with the same account on each device and your data follows you."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Do I need an account?","u":"docs/faq/#do-i-need-an-account","x":"You can try Expenity without one, and your data then stays in that browser only. An account (created with a passkey, no password) gives you sync between devices, a backup in case you lose a device, and shared wallets. See Account and sync."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"What is a passkey?","u":"docs/faq/#what-is-a-passkey","x":"Your fingerprint, face or device PIN, used instead of a password. Your device keeps the secret; Expenity only stores a public key. If you lose your passkeys, the recovery codes you saved at sign-up get you back in."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"I lost my passkey and my recovery codes.","u":"docs/faq/#i-lost-my-passkey-and-my-recovery-codes","x":"Email verification is not switched on yet, so there is no other way in. This is the trade-off of having no passwords. Save your recovery codes when you create the account, and add a second passkey on another device in More → Account and sync."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Will it remind me when the app is closed?","u":"docs/faq/#will-it-remind-me-when-the-app-is-closed","x":"Yes. With an account, turn on notifications in More → Reminders and calendar and Expenity sends one summary a day, at the hour you choose, even when the app is closed. On iPhone and iPad it needs the app added to the Home Screen. Without an account, download the calendar file and import it into your calendar. See Reminders."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Why not just connect to my bank?","u":"docs/faq/#why-not-just-connect-to-my-bank","x":"Reading a bank account would make Expenity handle bank credentials and sensitive data, which we would rather avoid. You tell it what you spend and earn; it tells you where it goes."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"What happens if I clear my browser data?","u":"docs/faq/#what-happens-if-i-clear-my-browser-data","x":"You are signed out and the data stored on that device is removed. With an account, sign in again with your passkey and everything syncs back. Without an account, clearing the data deletes it for good, so back up first. See Account and sync."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Where is my data stored? Can you read it?","u":"docs/faq/#where-is-my-data-stored-can-you-read-it","x":"On your device, and, if you have an account, on Expenity's server (Cloudflare) so it can sync. The connection is encrypted and only your passkey opens your account. Expenity does not look at your data, sell it or share it. See Privacy and your data."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Can several people share a wallet?","u":"docs/faq/#can-several-people-share-a-wallet","x":"Yes. Share a wallet with an invite code or link and choose whether people can edit or only view. See Shared wallets."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Does it support Greek?","u":"docs/faq/#does-it-support-greek","x":"Yes. Choose the language on the welcome screen, or in More → Appearance and language. The app, this help center and the website are in English and Greek, and the sentence box understands both. The Greek text is still being reviewed by a native speaker: please tell us what to improve."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Which currencies?","u":"docs/faq/#which-currencies","x":"Any currency by its three-letter code. A wallet has one currency, and totals are converted to your main currency with daily European Central Bank rates, marked with ≈. See Wallets and currencies."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Is the advice in \"Ideas\" financial advice?","u":"docs/faq/#is-the-advice-in-ideas-financial-advice","x":"No. Ideas are simple observations about your own data, such as \"this subscription costs €192 a year\". Decide for yourself."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"The sentence box got something wrong.","u":"docs/faq/#the-sentence-box-got-something-wrong","x":"It is a helper. Correct the fields below it. If you have a sentence that should work, please open an issue with the exact text."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"How do I report a bug or suggest something?","u":"docs/faq/#how-do-i-report-a-bug-or-suggest-something","x":"On GitHub."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"What is new?","u":"docs/faq/#what-is-new","x":"How do I get help while using a screen? Tap the pulsing ? at the top. It opens an animated tutorial in a popup. See Tutorials and help. Can I hide features I do not use? Yes. Pick Basic, Pro, Advanced or build your own on the Features page. See Features and plans. I started an expense and lost it. Look on Home or in More, Drafts. See Search, filters, drafts and recently deleted. I deleted something by mistake. More, Recently deleted keeps it for 30 days. How do I send feedback or report a problem? More, Help & about, Contact us. See Contact us. Which devices does Expenity work on? iPhone, iPad"}];